{"from": "artifact:cohors-ii-italica-diploma", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-10-01-italian-cohort", "grade": "C", "note": "The Roman army demonstrably included a cohort named 'Italica Civium Romanorum' (CIL XVI 106); but the diploma attests it stationed in Syria in 157 CE, an auxiliary cohort of Roman citizens, not the same unit in Judea in the 30s."}
{"from": "artifact:cohors-ii-italica-diploma", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:ac-10-01-italian-cohort", "note": "The unit's attested garrison is Syria, ca. 69-157 CE - after, and outside, the Judean setting of Acts 10:1; no source documents the cohort in Judea at all."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-2-18-7", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-10-01-caesarea-capital", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus (War 2.18.7) locates a Roman garrison at Caesarea in 66 CE, 'the greatest part of the Roman garrison ... raised out of Syria' - troops stationed at Caesarea, in a later decade, largely Syrian rather than Italian."}
{"from": "site:caesarea-maritima", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-10-01-caesarea-capital", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated city of Caesarea Maritima, with Herod's deep-sea harbour Sebastos (jetties built 22-15 BCE, dedicated 10/9 BCE), identified with the port city Acts 10:1 names; the Wikipedia article states it replaced Jerusalem as the province's civilian and military capital and was the governors' official residence. Regraded A to B by the architect (2026-11-16, titles ruling): the excavated site attests the city and harbour, not its status as capital and governors' residence, which the claim also states."}
{"from": "artifact:pilate-stone", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-10-01-caesarea-capital", "grade": "B", "note": "The prefect Pontius Pilate's own inscription was found at Caesarea Maritima (1961) - the same site named as the governors' residence; it names the office at the place, not the residence building itself."}
{"from": "artifact:aphrodisias-godfearers-stele", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-10-02-cornelius-godfearer", "grade": "C", "note": "The Aphrodisias stele (found 1975, published 1987) uses theosebeis as a technical category - Gentile sympathizers to Judaism existed as a class; 3rd-century Caria, not Cornelius in 30s Caesarea, and the 'God-fearers' category itself is debated in scholarship."}
{"from": "text:jubilees-22-separate", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-10-28-gentile-avoidance", "grade": "C", "note": "Jubilees 22 (2nd century BCE): 'Separate thyself from the nations, and eat not with them' - the avoidance of Gentile table-fellowship as an ideal in Second Temple Judaism, centuries before Acts 10; it does not attest Peter's words or the exact legal force."}
{"from": "text:leviticus-11", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:ac-10-14-food-laws", "note": "Biblical cross-reference: the clean/unclean animal list Peter's vision presupposes. A cross-reference never attests; the matrix cell stays none-found."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-15-331-caesarea", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-10-01-caesarea-capital", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus (Ant. 15.8): Herod rebuilt Strato's Tower and 'named it Cesarea' with the harbour, c. 22-10 BCE - the Herodian port city at the place Acts 10:1 names; it does not attest the later Roman-capital role."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-14-65", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-10-30-ninth-hour-prayer", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus (Ant. 14.65): the daily sacrifices were offered 'in the morning and about the ninth hour' - the ninth hour as a fixed prayer/sacrifice hour; it attests the hour of the temple rite, not that Cornelius prayed then."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-pesachim-5-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-10-30-ninth-hour-prayer", "grade": "C", "note": "Mishnah Pesahim 5:1 fixes the daily afternoon offering between eight and a half and nine and a half hours - the ninth-hour rite Luke calls the hour of prayer. Redacted c. 200 CE."}
{"from": "claim:ac-10-28-gentile-avoidance", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-10-05-not-gentiles-samaritans", "note": "The same separation logic at the level of mission policy: 'Go not into any way of the Gentiles' (Matt 10:5); Acts 10:28 states the everyday company-avoidance behind it."}
{"from": "claim:ac-10-28-gentile-avoidance", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:lk-09-52-53-samaritan-village-refused", "note": "Judean-Samaritan separation (Luke 9:52-53) is the intra-Jewish parallel to the Jew-Gentile company-avoidance of Acts 10:28."}
{"from": "claim:ac-10-30-ninth-hour-prayer", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:ac-03-01-ninth-hour-prayer", "note": "Same ninth-hour custom: Acts 3:1 has Peter and John going up to the temple at the hour of prayer, the ninth hour; Acts 10:30 has Cornelius praying at the ninth hour in his house."}
{"from": "claim:ac-10-01-cornelius-centurion", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-08-05-centurion-capernaum", "note": "Both centurions are sympathetic to Judaism; the Capernaum officer (Matt 8:5) serves Antipas, Cornelius a Roman cohort - different forces, same office."}
{"from": "site:joppa", "type": "identified_as", "to": "place:joppa", "note": "The ancient tell of Jaffa, today part of Tel Aviv-Yafo; identified without dispute."}
